From 5b3a9204ced94527eff5d7e56e197b7d907b5e51 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 23:23:35 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] ipa: libipa: agc: Limit how far an upper constraint darkens the frame

An upper constraint keeps the brightest part of the histogram below its
target by lowering the exposure of the whole frame. A light source or a
window in the scene then darkens everything else: a lamp in the
background of a video call, many times brighter than the face, pulls
the exposure down until the lamp itself no longer saturates.

Add an optional minRestRatio to the constraints in the tuning data: an
upper bound lowers the gain no further than to keep the mean of the
histogram below its qLo at this fraction of the mean luminance target.
Beyond that the highlights clip and the rest of the frame keeps a usable
brightness. For highlights smaller than 1 - qLo of the histogram those
pixels lie below the highlights, so their mean scales with the gain and
the limit comes from the histogram of the frame alone, with no
prediction of how the clipped pixels would change; larger highlights
fall partly among them and weaken the limit. The default, zero, keeps
the current behaviour; a lower bound ignores the value.

Signed-off-by: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
